<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.14.1: Distinguish Linear and Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01498</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.1: Distinguish Linear and Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9d0cab39-2dfc-440e-97bf-20a4906aa58a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Graph of line, origin O. Horizontal axis 0 to 7, by 1’s, labeled x. Vertical from 0 to 150, by 50’s, labeled y. Starts at 0 comma 120, appears to pass through 1 point 5 comma 125 and 7 comma 150.   ">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_16_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Which equation do you think the graph represents? Use the graph to support your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">\(y=120+(3.7) \cdot x\)</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\(y=120 \cdot (1.03)^x\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">
      All answers are acceptable if backed up by a reasonable explanation. Compare your answer:
      <list id="list-00001"><item>The graph is for \(y=120+3.7x\) because it appears linear. On the graph, when \(x=7\), the value of \(y\) appears to be a little less than 150. When \(x=7\), \(120+(3.7) \cdot 7=145.9\), so the slope of the line could be 3.7.</item><item>The graph is for \(y=120 \cdot (1.03)^x\). Multiplying 100 by 1.03 seven times, or \(100 \cdot (1.03)^7\), gives approximately 147.6, which more or less concurs with what is on the graph.</item><item>It is hard to tell. I tried finding the \(y\)-coordinate values for different values of  \(x\) using both equations, and they more or less agree with what is on the graph.</item></list>
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">What information might help you decide more easily whether the graph represents a linear or an exponential function?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Your answers may vary, but here are some examples:</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item>Exact coordinates of points on the graph with whole-number \(x\)-values</item>
            <item>A larger graphing window showing larger domain values so that the behavior of the function can be more visible</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>